<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Real Audiencia of Lima</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1542">1542</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Real <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> was the highest royal court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>, created in 1542 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws</a> and installed in Lima in 1544. Presided over by the viceroy, it heard appeals, advised on government and ruled the viceroyalty when the office of viceroy was vacant. It lasted until the end of Spanish rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in the 1820s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/real-audiencia-of-lima.jpg" alt="Real Audiencia of Lima" width="192" height="281" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Felipe Huaman Puma de Ayala · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grabado-Huaman-Poma-de-Ayala.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The viceroy, appointed by the king on the advice of the Council of the Indies, was the Crown's personal representative: he presided over the <b>Audiencia of Lima</b>, commanded the armed forces, supervised the treasury and exercised royal patronage over the Church.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
